="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Telecommunication" title="Telecommunication" class="mw-redirect">Telecommunications</a></td></tr><tr><th scope="row" class="infobox-label">Founded</th><td class="infobox-data">1972<span class="noprint"><span typeof="mw:Entity">;</span><span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span>54<span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span>years ago</span><span style="display:none"><span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span>(<span class="bday dtstart published updated">1972</span>)</span></td></tr><tr><th scope="row" class="infobox-label">Headquarters</th><td class="infobox-data label"><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Beirut" title="Beirut">Beirut</a>, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lebanon" title="Lebanon">Lebanon</a><span style="display:none" data-plural="0"></span></td></tr><tr><th scope="row" class="infobox-label"><div style="display: inline-block; line-height: 1.2em; padding: .1em 0; ">Area served</div></th><td class="infobox-data">Lebanon</td></tr><tr><th scope="row" class="infobox-label">Key<span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span>people</th><td class="infobox-data agent">Ahmad Oueidat (<a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chairman" title="Chairman" class="mw-redirect">Chairman</a>)</td></tr><tr><th scope="row" class="infobox-label">Website</th><td class="infobox-data"><a rel="mw:ExtLink nofollow" href="https://www.ogero.gov.lb/" class="external text">ogero.gov.lb</a></td></tr></tbody></table>
<span class="mw-empty-elt" about="#mwt5" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwBQ" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"external media\n","href":"./Template:External_media"},"params":{"topic":{"wt":""},"float":{"wt":"right"},"image1":{"wt":"[http://www.submarinecablemap.com/ Map] of sea cables (zoom over Lebanon to see all cables coming to the country and managed by Ogero)"}},"i":0}}]}'><link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1358620234" about="#mwt6" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Infobox/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""}}'/></span><table class="infobox" style="clear: right; float:right;margin:0 0 4px 1.5em" about="#mwt5" id="mwBg"><tbody><tr><th colspan="2" class="infobox-above" style="font-size:115%">External image</th></tr><tr><td colspan="2" class="infobox-full-data" style="text-align: left"><span class="noviewer" typeof="mw:File"><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Searchtool.svg" class="mw-file-description"><img resource="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Searchtool.svg" src="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/6/61/Searchtool.svg/20px-Searchtool.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail" decoding="async" loading="lazy" srcset="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/6/61/Searchtool.svg/40px-Searchtool.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail 2x" alt="image icon" data-file-width="512" data-file-height="512" data-file-type="drawing" height="16" width="16" class="mw-file-element"/></a></span> <a rel="mw:ExtLink nofollow" href="http://www.submarinecablemap.com/" class="external text">Map</a> of sea cables (zoom over Lebanon to see all cables coming to the country and managed by Ogero)</td></tr></tbody></table>

<p id="mwBw"><b id="mwCA">Ogero</b> (or <b id="mwCQ">OGERO</b>), which stands for "Organisme de Gestion et d'Exploitation de l'ex Radio Orient" (in <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/French_language" title="French language" id="mwCg">French</a>) is the fixed infrastructure operator in Lebanon, delivering voice and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Broadband" title="Broadband" id="mwCw">broadband</a> <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Internet" title="Internet" id="mwDA">internet</a> and data services to residential and Enterprises.<sup about="#mwt9" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-googleplay_1-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"googleplay"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-googleplay-1"}}'><a href="#cite_note-googleplay-1" id="mwDQ"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDg"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDw">[</span>1<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwEA">]</span></span></a></sup> It was founded by the Lebanese state in 1972. The company's head office is located in Beirut. The current Chairman is Ahmad Oueidat.<sup about="#mwt12" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-ogeromeet_2-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"ogeromeet"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-ogeromeet-2"}}'><a href="#cite_note-ogeromeet-2" id="mwEQ"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwEg"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwEw">[</span>2<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwFA">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwFQ">The <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/I-ME-WE" title="I-ME-WE" id="mwFg">I-ME-WE</a> (India-Middle East-Western Europe) <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Submarine_communications_cable" title="Submarine communications cable" id="mwFw">submarine communications cable system</a> was funded by a consortium of 9 companies from across the world including Ogero.<sup about="#mwt15" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-3" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-3"}}'><a href="#cite_note-3" id="mwGA"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwGQ"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwGg">[</span>3<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwGw">]</span></span></a></sup> The I-ME-WE cable system comprises three optical fiber cable pairs and 2 trunk lines. It has been operational since 2009 with <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tripoli,_Lebanon" title="Tripoli, Lebanon" id="mwHA">Tripoli, Lebanon</a> being connected in November 2011.<sup about="#mwt19" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-4" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-4"}}'><a href="#cite_note-4" id="mwHQ"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwHg"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwHw">[</span>4<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwIA">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwIQ">In July 2018, Ogero enabled <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Internet_Protocol_version_6" title="Internet Protocol version 6" class="mw-redirect" id="mwIg">Internet Protocol version 6</a> (IPv6) support for DSL users and for private operators.<sup about="#mwt22" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-5" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-5"}}'><a href="#cite_note-5" id="mwIw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwJA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwJQ">[</span>5<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwJg">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwJw">On 5 November 2019, during the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2019–20_Lebanese_protests" title="2019–20 Lebanese protests" class="mw-redirect" id="mwKA">2019–20 Lebanese protests</a>, protesters in <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nabatieh" title="Nabatieh" id="mwKQ">Nabatieh</a>, shut down Ogero office despite state-exerted political pressure towards the protesters in this region.<sup about="#mwt25" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-6" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-6"}}'><a href="#cite_note-6" id="mwKg"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwKw"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwLA">[</span>6<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwLQ">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwLg">On 12 March 2020, the government announced that internet service through Ogero would be boosted through the end of April, to encourage users to stay home to protect themselves from the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/COVID-19_pandemic_in_Lebanon" title="COVID-19 pandemic in Lebanon" id="mwLw">coronavirus pandemic</a>.<sup about="#mwt28" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-7" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-7"}}'><a href="#cite_note-7" id="mwMA"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwMQ"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwMg">[</span>7<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwMw">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwNA">Through its network of central offices, the state-owned company Ogero provides the "backbone" telecommunication infrastructure which distributes internet capacity throughout the country. The actual distribution occurs via Data Service Providers (DSPs) and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Internet_service_provider" title="Internet service provider" id="mwNQ">Internet service providers</a> (ISPs). Ogero also doubles up as an ISP. Ogero distributes internet to all providers, while acting as the dominant ISP itself.</p>
<figure class="mw-default-size" typeof="mw:File/Thumb" id="mwNg"><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Old_logo_OGERO_(Lebanon)_2014.png" class="mw-file-description" id="mwNw"><img resource="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Old_logo_OGERO_(Lebanon)_2014.png" src="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/0/02/Old_logo_OGERO_%28Lebanon%29_2014.png/250px-Old_logo_OGERO_%28Lebanon%29_2014.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail" decoding="async" loading="lazy" srcset="//upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/0/02/Old_logo_OGERO_%28Lebanon%29_2014.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x" data-file-width="275" data-file-height="362" data-file-type="bitmap" height="329" width="250" class="mw-file-element" id="mwOA"/></a><figcaption id="mwOQ">Former logo from 2014.</figcaption></figure>

</section><section data-mw-section-id="1" id="mwOg" aria-labelledby="Speed"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading2"><h2 id="Speed">Speed</h2><span class="mw-editsection"><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">[</span><a href="/w/index.php?title=Ogero&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1" title="Edit section: Speed"><span>edit</span></a><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">]</span></span></div>

<p id="mwOw">As of July 2025, the fixed broadband speed provided by Ogero is ranked 141 out of more than 190 countries according to <a rel="mw:ExtLink nofollow" href="http://Speedtest.net" class="external text" id="mwPA">Speedtest.net</a>.
The average speed is 16.44 Mbps down and 13.56 Mbps up.<sup about="#mwt31" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-8" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-8"}}'><a href="#cite_note-8" id="mwPQ"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwPg"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwPw">[</span>8<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwQA">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

</section><section data-mw-section-id="2" id="mwQQ" aria-labelledby="See_also"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading2"><h2 id="See_also">See also</h2><span class="mw-editsection"><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">[</span><a href="/w/index.php?title=Ogero&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2" title="Edit section: See also"><span>edit</span></a><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">]</span></span></div>
